    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so just to be 100% sure i got the insurance part right... the seller pays for it, but the buyer is the one who actually benefits if something happens at sea, right? and what kind of insurance coverage are they actually required to provide under CIF? is it just minimum coverage or something else?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You've hit on a very important nuance there, which is often where disputes arise! The seller *pays* for the insurance under CIF, but because the risk transferred to you (the buyer) once the goods are loaded, you are the one who needs to *claim* on that insurance if something goes wrong at sea. It's a bit counterintuitive, but the contract dictates who pays for the policy, not necessarily who benefits from the claim in the event of a loss after risk transfer.\n\nRegarding the coverage level, CIF requires the seller to procure at least the minimum coverage available under the Institute Cargo Clauses (C). This is the basic level, which covers perils like sinking or collision, but it is *not* comprehensive. If you want more robust protection, you, the buyer, should consider purchasing additional insurance yourself once the goods are on board.\n\nThis minimum coverage is often the most overlooked part, so make sure you understand what \"Institute Cargo Clauses (C)\" actually covers before signing off on this. Don't let the seller's basic insurance be your only safety net!\n\nIf you're worried about the risk, you might want to negotiate for a higher level of coverage, perhaps Institute Cargo Clauses (A",
      "source": "target",
