    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah that makes sense. so if the seller only provides the basic (C) coverage, there's definitely a gap in protection for the buyer. i'm a bit worried about the shipment being delayed or damaged by things like theft or water damage during transit. how would i go about ensuring those specific risks are covered, and is that something i just add to the contract or do it separately?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right to be worried; the basic Clause (C) is often insufficient for modern shipping risks. To cover things like theft or extensive water damage, you need to upgrade the insurance coverage beyond the minimum required by CIF. You can't just tack it onto the seller's CIF obligation; you need to arrange it separately, usually by purchasing an additional policy.\n\nThe best way to do this is to ensure your own insurance policy, which you arrange as the buyer, covers the goods from the moment they are loaded onto the vessel onward. You would typically look for coverage equivalent to Institute Cargo Clauses (A) or even broader \"All Risks\" coverage. This ensures that if a sea-borne peril occurs, *you* are the one filing the claim, and you have the strongest possible backing.\n\nIt’s a proactive move, and it’s wise to be cautious. Don't rely solely on the seller's basic insurance if your business operations depend on those components arriving safely.\n\nIf you're looking at this from a pure contractual standpoint, you can certainly add a clause stating that while the seller arranges CIF, the buyer retains the right to purchase supplemental insurance, but the actual purchase must be done by you",
